    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "P. F. PALMER. LITH.\nLENGTH OF KEE\nLENGTH ON DECK\nLENGTH OVERARI\nDIMNSIONS\n..175 FEET 6 IN.\n180 FEET\n207 F 321\nnored according to and of Congress in cha year 1855 by N. Curries in the Clace Offin of the Didid Cause of Name Yout\nI. Dant D. Westervel Cog. builder, of the New Yorke\nCLIPPER SHIP \"CONTEST\nThis print is rospoctfully dadicatad by the Publisher.\nLITH. BY N. CURRIEr\nROPANTH OF REAM\nDEPTH OF HOLD\nTONNAGE PER REGISTER\nBUILT BY D. D. WESTERYELT, NEW TORA 1634\n36 PEST",
      "The front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print.\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Museum of the City of New York.\nThe story of \"Clipper Ships\" is the story of America itself! The New England \"Yankee\" ship-\nbuilders built a merchant fleet that was a decisive factor in the American Revolution and the War\nof 1812. These merchant fleets gathered the wealth that founded our great banks and factories, built\nour railroads and pushed our frontiers ever Westward. It was these same merchant fleets that paid\nthe duties that enabled Thomas Jefferson to purchase the Louisiana Territory. They made our\nmerchant marine the greatest afloat, and in the process, produced the greatest, the swiftest, the\nmost beautiful craft the world had ever seen ... the American Clipper Ship. By the year 1853 the\ndemand for clippers reached frenzied heights... as many as five were launched in one day. The\n\"Contest,\" built to satisfy the rush for gold in California, broke many speed records fighting her way\naround the horn to San Francisco and from there to the Orient.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the early clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to all the far places of the world ... to Japan... to Australia... and, in the days of the great\ngold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California. Our current\nequipment... the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century ... consists of the fihest commercial\naircraft ever developed—aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record for safety,\nspeed and service.\n*CUPPER TRADE MARK-REG. U.S. PAT. OFFICE - COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS ING\n2000.\n058.0317\nLITHO IN U.SA-8739-0393"
    ],
